      "html": "<nav class=\"ds-rail\"><a class=\"ds-nav ds-nav--current\" href=\"#\">Home<span>2</span></a><a class=\"ds-nav\" href=\"#\">Map<span>23</span></a></nav>",
      "css": ".ds-rail{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;width:200px;padding:16px;background:var(--rail,#1F4D45)}.ds-nav{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:10px;padding:8px 10px;border-radius:8px;color:var(--rail-foreground,#D5E5DF);font:400 14px 'Schibsted Grotesk',sans-serif;text-decoration:none;transition:background .15s}.ds-nav span{margin-left:auto;font:400 12px 'JetBrains Mono',monospace;color:var(--rail-muted,#BCD5CC)}.ds-nav:hover{background:rgb(42 95 85 / .6);color:#fff}.ds-nav:focus-visible{outline:2px solid var(--rail-ring,#E3A72F);outline-offset:2px}.ds-nav--current{background:var(--rail-active,#2A5F55);color:#fff;font-weight:600}.ds-nav--current span{color:#fff}"
    }
  ],
  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Gallery Guide",
    "overview": "Learning a codebase is walking an exhibition. Every user journey is an exhibit with numbered stops, and the\ndashboard is the gallery: a painted viridian wall on the left (the rail), a gallery-white floor for the work,\nand ochre audio-guide roundels that number every stop. Names read like wall labels: short, bold, with a quiet\ncaption under them. Real paths, lines and SQL sit beside them in a monospace, because the product's promise is\n\"follow the data\", not abstractions.\n\nThis is an Operate surface: a developer spends 20 to 60 minutes in it next to a terminal. Character lives in\nprecise details (the roundels, the lane hues, the hairline count strip, the confidence of every connector), not\nin decoration. It is light-only, dense but calm, and every number lines up. It refuses the grey stat-card\ndeveloper dashboard with a folder tree.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Viridian rail as the painted wall; gallery-white ground; white panels with 1px hairlines.",
      "Ochre numbered roundels are the one signature; nothing else uses ochre fill.",
      "Four lane hues (UI blue, API viridian, Code stone, DB umber) tell where a stop lives, on every screen.",
      "Connectors carry confidence: solid for exact, thin for a path-param match, dashed for a guess. A guess is never drawn as fact.",
      "Oxblood is reserved for errors and hops codeloom could not follow.",
      "Schibsted Grotesk for words, JetBrains Mono only for code, paths, lines, routes and SQL."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The One Signature Rule",
        "body": "Ochre fill means a numbered stop and nothing else. Never use it for a badge, a chart",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Honest Roundel Rule",
        "body": "On Home, roundels are hollow and ringed in their lane hue: Home names the stops, it",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Oxblood Rule",
        "body": "Oxblood appears only for errors and broken or unresolved hops, and always with a second",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Mono-Means-Data Rule",
        "body": "Monospace only for code, paths, line numbers, routes, SQL, repo ids and counts in",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Tabular Numbers Rule",
        "body": "Numbers that line up use tabular figures, but only on the number itself: never",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Break-at-Slash Rule",
        "body": "Paths and code names wrap after `/`, `_`, `-`, at a dot before a long part and at",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Flat-By-Default Rule",
        "body": "Panels carry a hairline, never a hairline plus a wide soft shadow. A shadow",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "**Do** number every stop with an ochre roundel on Journey and Map, and with a hollow lane-ringed roundel on Home.",
      "**Do** keep lane hues consistent across Home roundels, Journey lanes, Map layer cards and the rail repo dots.",
      "**Do** draw a hop's confidence in its line (solid, thin, dashed) and explain a guess in words.",
      "**Do** wrap paths at `/` and names at humps; give the full text as a tooltip.",
      "**Do** keep focus visible: when a class removes the outline, add `outline-solid` back on `focus-visible` (Tailwind v4's `outline-none` zeroes the outline style).",
      "**Do** keep text at 4.5:1 or better; captions use Slate Caption, never a lighter grey.",
      "**Do** respect reduced motion: transitions are 150ms colour/opacity changes and switch off under `prefers-reduced-motion`."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "**Don't** use ochre fill for anything but a stop, and don't fill Home roundels until progress exists.",
      "**Don't** show placeholders or \"coming soon\" chrome for Tour, Progress, Views, spec badges or understood %.",
      "**Don't** use oxblood without a dashed edge or the word that says what failed.",
      "**Don't** truncate a stop name to one line with an ellipsis, or break a path in the middle of a segment.",
      "**Don't** set monospace on headings or UI words, or `tabular-nums` on whole sentences.",
      "**Don't** add a dark theme, CDN fonts or remote assets: the dashboard is light-only and loads nothing beyond 127.0.0.1.",
      "**Don't** add eyebrow labels, stat cards with icons, gradient text or border-left accent stripes."
    ]
  }
